WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.488
IEC 62443 organizes technical security objectives into seven foundational requirements.

00:00:05.488 --> 00:00:13.839
Together they provide a balanced view of identity, authorization, integrity, confidentiality, communication, detection, response, and availability.

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:22.191
The required strength of each objective depends on the zone, conduit, risk, and selected security level.

00:00:22.191 --> 00:00:30.065
Identification and authentication control establishes who or what is requesting access and verifies that identity.

00:00:30.065 --> 00:00:37.462
Use control determines what an authenticated user, device, or process is allowed to do.

00:00:37.462 --> 00:00:47.722
Industrial systems may have technical limitations, so architecture and managed gateways may need to enforce controls that individual devices cannot.

00:00:47.722 --> 00:00:54.641
System integrity protects systems, software, configurations, and communications from unauthorized or accidental change.

00:00:54.641 --> 00:01:01.084
Data confidentiality protects information from unauthorized disclosure where that disclosure creates risk.

00:01:01.084 --> 00:01:09.435
Confidentiality matters in OT, but it must be implemented without undermining required visibility, timing, or recovery.

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:14.923
Restricted data flow limits communication between zones and through conduits.

00:01:14.923 --> 00:01:21.843
It supports segmentation and containment by allowing necessary flows while denying unnecessary paths.

00:01:21.843 --> 00:01:30.194
Timely response to events addresses the ability to detect, record, communicate, and respond to security-relevant activity.

00:01:30.194 --> 00:01:34.728
Useful monitoring requires ownership, context, and practiced procedures.

00:01:34.728 --> 00:01:41.170
Resource availability protects the capacity and functions needed for safe, reliable operation.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:47.613
This includes resilience against resource exhaustion, component failure, and disruptive security controls.

00:01:47.613 --> 00:01:52.623
Redundancy, backup, recovery, and graceful degradation may all contribute.

00:01:52.623 --> 00:01:57.634
The foundational requirements should be engineered as a system.

00:01:57.634 --> 00:02:04.077
Strong authentication without availability planning can lock operators out during an emergency.

00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:08.610
Excellent logging without response ownership produces little value.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:16.007
The objective is a set of mutually supporting controls aligned with the industrial process.
